# Programs held out of the differential run. One basename per line (no
# extension), each with the reason it is not run; remove an entry once
# that reason no longer holds. A reason that measurement contradicts is
# worse than no entry at all -- it is read as a known cost and stops
# anyone looking.
#
# ch8  -- does not finish on a narrow-object build. Its shfill figures are
#         finely-tessellated 3-D meshes painted a triangle at a time
#         through a DeviceN colour reduction in PostScript, and doing so
#         holds enough composites live at once to exhaust the 524,287
#         entity numbers a narrow object's 19-bit ent field can name. The
#         run stops about seven seconds in, having written eight of the
#         file's twelve pages, under a VMerror out of setcolor that no
#         stopped context can catch.
#
#         That is a limit of the narrow build, not of the renderer and
#         not of its speed: a large-object build, whose ent field is 31
#         bits, renders all twelve pages correctly in about 170 seconds,
#         well inside the per-file budget. So there is nothing here
#         waiting on a mesh rasterizer, and the entry stays only until
#         the entity-number cap is addressed.
#
#         It is held out rather than run and reported because the manner
#         of the failure is not something to depend on. The evaluator
#         carries on past a program that dies under a PostScript error,
#         counting the pages it could not compare, but a signal death
#         fails the corpus outright -- and an allocator that has run out
#         of entity numbers is not a thing to promise which of the two it
#         will do. A gate that passes only while a known defect keeps
#         failing politely is not reporting on the renderer.
ch8
